{"reference":"Isaiah 64","book":"Isaiah","chapter":64,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"A communal voice within Isaiah","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah uses more than one kind of voice, including divine speech, communal speech, and narration, so the speaker must be identified passage by passage. Here the repeated “we,” “our,” and “us” frame the chapter as a communal plea: the speakers confess shared wrongdoing, describe the ruin of their cities and place of praise, and appeal together to the Lord as their Father and potter.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_064_3c5aeb73745cea36702c0ded"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}